    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d170">

                <pb facs="0381" n="123" xml:id="pg123"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1501</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-24T14:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 24, 1915, 2 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 25, 9 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1680. Referring to your propositions in identic note.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d170-f1"/> From private information think Germany inclined to accept
                    but will probably want raw material to enter as well as food for civil
                    population. Copper may be subject of contention but there is ample copper here
                    for war purposes and it is only needed for manufactures. There will also be
                    objection to agents of American Government superintending food distribution as
                    this might involve some surrender of sovereignty but this can be obviated by
                    creation of an American commission or something of sort similar to Belgian or
                    one proposed for Poland and of which I could be chairman personally and not
                    officially.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
